# AI Interview Coach — Challenge Retrospective

**AB Talks 60-Day Claude AI Challenge — 10-Day Capstone**

## Timeline: Day 1 → Day 10

**Day 1 — Discovery.** Started with no fixed idea, discovered AI Interview Coach through a structured interview: CS background, Python/Streamlit/AI-API strengths, interest in HR/recruitment problems. Landed on a candidate-facing interview coach after explicitly scoping out recruiter-facing features and multi-user auth to protect the 10-day timeline. Deliverables: PRD, 9-day Implementation Blueprint, Pitch Deck.

**Day 2 — System Design.** Finalized architecture (Streamlit monolith with UI/Service/AI/Data layering), SQLite schema, internal service-layer "API," and low-fi wireframes. Introduced `core/services.py` as a design refinement — not in the original Day 1 plan, but added specifically so the "API Design" deliverable would mean something for a Streamlit app with no real HTTP endpoints. This one decision paid off repeatedly later.

**Day 3 — Foundation.** Environment setup hit its first real obstacle: `pandas==2.2.2` failed to build on Windows/Python 3.13 (no prebuilt wheel). Fixed by bumping to `2.2.3`. Then, requesting a Claude API key revealed Anthropic now requires prepaid credits — no guaranteed free trial. Rather than block on a purchase decision, built `MockProvider` as a stand-in behind the `AIProvider` interface designed the day before. This turned a blocker into validation of the architecture.

**Day 4 — Question Generation.** Built the real input UI (role/JD/resume), resume PDF parsing, and `core/services.py`'s `start_session()`. Verified all three input paths (role-only, +JD, +resume) against `MockProvider` with zero real AI cost.

**Day 5 — The Pivot.** Attempted real AI integration — discovered the Anthropic prepaid-credit requirement was a hard blocker without spending money. Made the explicit decision to switch to **Groq** (free, no credit card, Llama 3.3 70B) instead. Implemented `GroqProvider` against the *exact same* `AIProvider` interface from Day 2 — no other file needed to change. Also debugged a real dependency conflict (`groq==0.11.0` needed `httpx==0.27.2`, not the latest). This was the day the Day 2 architecture bet was fully validated.

**Day 6 — MVP Completion & First Deployment Attempt.** Built History and Dashboard pages, added the required footer, and attempted deployment. Hit a wall: Streamlit Cloud's build environment failed to install `pandas`. Multiple fix attempts were made but not visually confirmed before the session ended — an honest, documented "code-complete but unverified" state rather than a false completion claim.

**Day 7 — The Real Root Cause.** Picked up Day 6's unresolved deployment. What looked like a pandas problem was actually Streamlit Cloud running **Python 3.14** — a brand-new runtime with poor early package-wheel coverage. Traced through six cascading failures (pandas → pillow → pyarrow, each needing a version bump, each revealing the next) before finding the actual fix: pin Python 3.11 via the platform's dashboard setting, not just a `runtime.txt` file. The app went live. Then completed the day's actual scheduled work — a full visual polish pass (custom theme, icons, consistent states) — and reconfirmed it carried over to production.

**Day 8 — Production Hardening.** Conducted a formal QA/security/performance review before writing code, surfacing 8 real findings (from a critical "one bad AI field discards a whole evaluation" bug down to code-quality nits). Rewrote the AI-response handling with per-field safe coercion instead of all-or-nothing fallback, added guaranteed database connection cleanup, added indexes, cached the AI provider client, and wrote 28 automated tests — including a direct regression test for the score-clamping bug. All 28 passed on the first full run.

**Day 9 — Release Readiness.** Wrote the professional README, added an MIT license, configured GitHub repo metadata, and made a deliberate, documented decision to skip social/OG-tag metadata for v1.0 rather than risk an unsupported Streamlit workaround. Hit one more small snag — GitHub's secret-scanning push protection caught the Groq key accidentally in an early README draft — a good final reminder of the secret-hygiene discipline maintained since Day 3.

**Day 10 — Graduation.** Final review, portfolio materials, and v1.0.0 release.

## Major Technical Decisions & Pivots

1. **`core/services.py` as a Day 2 refinement** — not originally planned, added to make the internal API design meaningful. Became the single seam that absorbed every later change.
2. **MockProvider as a first-class fallback, not just a placeholder** (Day 3) — turned a payment blocker into zero lost time.
3. **Anthropic → Groq pivot** (Day 5) — the single biggest real-world adaptation of the whole build, and the clearest proof the `AIProvider` abstraction was worth designing on Day 2.
4. **Root-causing over patching** (Day 7) — six failed fixes before finding the actual Python-version issue; the lesson was reading the *full* log, not just the last error line.
5. **Per-field AI response validation over all-or-nothing** (Day 8) — a subtle but important correctness fix: real AI content should survive one bad field, not get discarded entirely.

